M="biochem-molecular-2"
SUB="Molecular"
T={
"code":[("That a given codon always specifies the same amino acid makes the genetic code:",["Unambiguous","Degenerate","Overlapping","Reversible"],0,"Specificity runs one way only: a codon has one meaning."),
 ("Which two amino acids are each specified by only one codon?",["Methionine and tryptophan","Arginine and leucine","Serine and proline","Glycine and alanine"],0,"Arginine, by contrast, has six codons."),
 ("In human mitochondria, UGA codes for tryptophan rather than acting as a stop signal. This shows that the genetic code is:",["Nearly but not entirely universal","Ambiguous in certain human tissues","Overlapping in organelles","Read without a fixed frame"],0,"Specificity has been conserved since early evolution, with only slight differences in how the code is translated.",2)],
"genereg":[("An enhancer sequence may be located:",["Upstream or downstream of the gene","Only immediately upstream","Only inside the first intron","Only on the opposite strand"],0,"It acts at a distance by looping the DNA back toward the promoter."),
 ("A silencer resembles an enhancer except that it:",["Decreases gene expression","Acts only over short distances","Binds RNA polymerase itself","Is found only in prokaryotes"],0,"Both act at long range; they differ in direction of effect."),
 ("Before transcription factors can reach their binding sites, the chromatin in that region must first be:",["Remodelled into a relaxed form","Replicated a second time","Cleaved by an endonuclease","Methylated at its CpG islands"],0,"Condensed heterochromatin physically blocks access, so acetylation and related changes must open it first.",2)],
"operon":[("Allolactose turns the lac operon on by:",["Inactivating the repressor protein","Binding RNA polymerase directly","Activating the CAP protein","Removing the operator sequence"],0,"It changes the repressor conformation so it can no longer bind the operator."),
 ("Catabolite repression occurs when glucose is present because:",["Adenylyl cyclase is inactive","The repressor cannot be released","The operator sequence is deleted","Lactose cannot enter the cell"],0,"Without cAMP, CAP does not bind and transcription is inefficient."),
 ("The lac operon is transcribed maximally under which condition?",["Lactose present and glucose absent","Lactose present and glucose present","Lactose absent and glucose absent","Lactose absent and glucose present"],0,"The repressor must be released and CAP-cAMP must be bound; one alone is not enough.",2)],
"rnapol":[("Which RNA polymerase synthesises mRNA?",["RNA polymerase II","RNA polymerase I","RNA polymerase III","Mitochondrial RNA polymerase"],0,"Polymerase I makes rRNA and polymerase III makes tRNA and 5S rRNA."),
 ("Alpha-amanitin from the death cap mushroom inhibits:",["RNA polymerase II","RNA polymerase I","DNA polymerase III","Reverse transcriptase"],0,"It forms a tight complex with the enzyme, halting mRNA and then protein synthesis."),
 ("Rifampin does not affect human transcription because:",["Eukaryotic polymerase does not bind it","It cannot enter human cells","It is inactivated by liver enzymes","Human cells lack a promoter region"],0,"The drug distorts the bacterial enzyme so it cannot initiate, but the eukaryotic enzyme is unaffected.",2)],
"rnaproc":[("Which sequences are invariant at the two ends of an intron?",["GU at the 5' end and AG at the 3' end","AG at the 5' end and GU at the 3' end","AU at the 5' end and GC at the 3' end","CA at the 5' end and UG at the 3' end"],0,"Mutations here abolish correct splicing."),
 ("The lariat structure formed during splicing arises from an unusual bond involving:",["The 2'-OH of a branch site adenosine","The 3'-OH of the upstream exon","The 5' cap guanosine residue","The poly-A tail"],0,"That 2'-OH attacks the phosphate at the splice donor site."),
 ("A patient has antibodies to small nuclear ribonucleoproteins. These particles normally function in:",["Removal of introns from pre-mRNA","Addition of the poly-A tail","Transport of mRNA to the cytosol","Charging of transfer RNA"],0,"The snRNPs assemble with the transcript into the spliceosome; anti-Smith antibodies are specific for lupus.",3)],
"trna":[("Every transfer RNA ends at its 3' terminus with:",["CCA","AUG","GGU","UAA"],0,"The amino acid is esterified to this terminal adenosine."),
 ("Transfer RNAs that accept the same amino acid are called:",["Isoaccepting","Isoelectric","Isomorphic","Isoenzymatic"],0,"Degeneracy of the code means several codons, and so several tRNAs, serve one amino acid."),
 ("Why is aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase the critical accuracy checkpoint of translation?",["The ribosome checks only the anticodon","It is the slowest step of translation","It is the only step that uses ATP","It proofreads the messenger RNA"],0,"The ribosome never verifies which amino acid is attached, so an error made here passes unchallenged into the protein.",3)],
"transl":[("The three ribosomal sites are occupied in which order by a transfer RNA?",["A then P then E","P then A then E","E then A then P","A then E then P"],0,"Aminoacyl, peptidyl, exit."),
 ("Peptide bond formation is catalysed by peptidyl transferase, which is:",["A ribozyme","A metalloprotein","A serine protease","A GTP-binding protein"],0,"The ribosomal RNA itself provides the catalytic activity."),
 ("Aminoglycosides can be used safely in humans because they bind a ribosomal subunit that is:",["30S, absent from human ribosomes","50S, absent from human ribosomes","60S, present only in bacteria","40S, present only in bacteria"],0,"Bacterial ribosomes are 70S (50S plus 30S) while human cytosolic ribosomes are 80S (60S plus 40S).",3)],
"posttr":[("C-peptide is produced during the processing of:",["Proinsulin","Proglucagon","Procollagen","Prothrombin"],0,"Its level distinguishes endogenous insulin from injected insulin."),
 ("Hydroxylation of proline and lysine residues in collagen requires:",["Vitamin C","Vitamin K","Vitamin B12","Vitamin D"],0,"Deficiency gives the poor wound healing and bleeding gums of scurvy."),
 ("An infant has coarse facial features, stiff joints and markedly raised plasma lysosomal enzyme levels, with those enzymes nearly absent inside the cells. The defect lies in:",["Mannose-6-phosphate tagging","Chaperone-assisted protein folding","Carboxylation of glutamate residues","Proteolytic trimming of prohormones"],0,"Without that tag the enzymes are secreted instead of being sent to the lysosome, which is I-cell disease.",3)],
}
